  :adr/body "# ADR 2799006330: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 6330 Subsistence Mixed Crop and Livestock Farmers farm record-keeping/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 6330\n-- Subsistence Mixed Crop and Livestock Farmers** only, part of Wave3\n(physical-production, `kotoba.occupation.wave` sub-major \"63\"),\nfollowing on from the already-landed 6310 (crop) and 6320 (livestock)\nsiblings in this same batch.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"6330\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"6330\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Subsistence Mixed Crop and\nLivestock Farmers\" -- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to\nthis ADR the entry's `:maturity` was `:spec` with\n`:required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]` and no\n`:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6330` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before\nscaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the\n`6330` entry to `:implemented`.\n\n**This actor carries THREE distinct guardrail dimensions at once,\nmore than either of its sibling actors alone: subsistence mixed crop\nand livestock farmers grow crops AND raise animals for household\nconsumption, combining the livelihood-vulnerability dimension\n(crop/livestock loss directly threatens household food security) with\nan animal-welfare dimension AND agronomic-decision authority.** A\nwrongful automated decision could compound harm across any of these\naxes at once -- an agronomic misjudgment (wrong planting/harvest\ntiming) harming the crop, a treatment/welfare misjudgment harming the\nanimal, or a livelihood-affecting decision made without the farmer's\nown consent. The controlling precedent is **ADR-2607152500** (Wave4\nrollout start amendment), which established that decisions directly\ntouching safety, dignity or subsistence must always exclude the closed\nop allowlist and always escalate to a human, not merely be gated\nbehind a confidence threshold. This ADR applies that guardrail shape\nhere, for BOTH dimensions simultaneously: no op anywhere in this\nactor's closed allowlist may finalize a planting/harvest-timing\nagronomic decision OR an animal-treatment/welfare/breeding decision,\nand none may ever override the farmer's own judgment about their\nhousehold's crops or livestock.\n\nThe reference implementations mirrored for module SHAPE and for both\nhalves of the combined guardrail pattern were cloned and read in full\nthis session:\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6320`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6320)\n(Subsistence Livestock Farmers: `husbandry.store`/`husbandry.advisor`/\n`husbandry.governor`/`husbandry.actor` split, the animal-treatment/\nwelfare/breeding scope-exclusion pattern, and the registered-AND-\nverified farmer/holding provenance shape) and\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6310`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6310)\n(Subsistence Crop Farmers: `subsistencefarm.store`/`subsistencefarm.advisor`/\n`subsistencefarm.governor`/`subsistencefarm.actor` split, the\nplanting/harvest-timing agronomic scope-exclusion pattern). Both use\nthe same `langgraph.graph` StateGraph shape, closed-allowlist +\nprovenance + content-based scope-exclusion safety pattern, and full\ndoc set. This actor (namespace `mixedfarm.*`) merges both guardrail\nhalves into a single mixed-holding actor rather than picking one.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6330` as an OSS\ndocumentation/logistics-coordination actor for subsistence mixed crop\nand livestock farmers, following the SAME governed-actor architecture\nas every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Farm\nOperations Advisor ⊣ MixedFarmGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph`\nStateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned\nidentically to both reference repos' `deps.edn` -- this is a different\nlibrary coordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated\n`20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor, human-in-\nthe-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit\nledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **farm record-keeping/logistics\ncoordination ONLY, never agronomic-decision or animal-handling/\ntreatment authority** -- it must NEVER be designed to exercise,\nsimulate exercising, or propose exercising ANY of: a planting/\nharvest-timing agronomic decision, an animal-treatment/welfare/\nbreeding decision, or an override of the farmer's own judgment about\ntheir household's crops or livestock. These are not merely gated\nbehind escalation -- they are structurally absent from the\nop-allowlist entirely, and enforced a second, independent way via a\ncontent-based scope-exclusion check over free text.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- planting/harvest/feeding-schedule/animal-\n  condition-check-in data logging (metadata only -- work id, work\n  type, detail, timestamp -- NEVER a planting/harvest-timing decision,\n  a treatment decision, a welfare disposition, or a breeding decision)\n- `:schedule-farm-operation` -- household labor/task scheduling\n  proposal (advance scheduling logistics only -- never a planting/\n  harvest directive, a treatment directive, or a real-time farmer-\n  judgment override)\n- `:flag-livelihood-concern` -- surface a crop-failure/animal-welfare/\n  injury-risk/food-security concern, ALWAYS escalates immediately,\n  NEVER auto-commit-eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- seeds/feed/tools procurement proposal,\n  escalates above a registered per-holding cost threshold\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles**: a\nplanting/harvest-timing agronomic decision, an animal-treatment/\nwelfare/breeding decision, or an override of the farmer's own judgment\nabout their household's crops or livestock. These are **structurally\nabsent from the op-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind\nescalation -- an op such as `:finalize-planting-decision` or\n`:authorize-veterinary-treatment` cannot even be proposed by the\nreference mock advisor (its `infer` function only ever emits one of\nthe four allowlist ops, driven by a `case` expression with no default\nbranch producing any other op shape), and if any code path (a forged\nproposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever produced such\nan op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check (`:op-not-allowed`)\nrejects it unconditionally and permanently. Verified two ways: (a)\nunit tests directly against `governor/check` for seventeen\nconcretely-named forbidden op fixtures\n(`mixedfarm.governor/scope-excluded-ops`, combining 6310's five\nagronomic exclusions with 6320's twelve animal-treatment/welfare/\nbreeding exclusions), and (b) an END-TO-END test that drives each of\nthose same seventeen ops through a ROGUE advisor and the FULL\n`langgraph.graph` actor graph (not just the governor's pure check\nfunction), proving the StateGraph itself always resolves to `:hold`\nwith zero records committed even when the advisor node itself is\ncompromised\n(`mixedfarm.actor-test/holds-every-scope-excluded-op-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`).\n\n### Seven HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Farmer unregistered/unverified** -- any proposal must resolve to\n   an independently `:verified?` farmer/holding record. Never trusts\n   the proposal's own claim of who the farmer is. Directly implements\n   the task's \"farmer/holding record must be independently verified/\n   registered before any action\" invariant for the farmer half.\n2. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance -- the\n   governor never dispatches hardware, never performs farm labor,\n   never handles the animals directly).\n3. **Op not allowed** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op allowlist\n   is a HARD, PERMANENT block. This independently rejects any attempt\n   to slip in a planting/harvest-decision-shaped, breeding-fitness/\n   veterinary-treatment/welfare-disposition-shaped, or farmer-\n   judgment-override-shaped op.\n4. **Holding basis** -- `:log-work-record`, `:schedule-farm-operation`\n   and `:coordinate-supply-order` must cite a REGISTERED AND VERIFIED\n   holding matching the farmer's own holding (`:missing-holding-id` /\n   `:unknown-holding` / `:holding-unverified` / `:holding-mismatch`).\n   `:flag-livelihood-concern` is deliberately EXEMPT from this check --\n   it is the channel by which a brand-new holding, or an urgent crop/\n   livestock/food-security concern with no holding on file yet, is\n   surfaced for human intake, so it must not be blocked on the very\n   holding record it exists to help flag.\n5. **Work-record decision forbidden** -- `:log-work-record` is a\n   planting/harvest/feeding-schedule/animal-condition-check-in\n   metadata record ONLY. Any proposal carrying a planting/harvest-\n   timing decision field OR an animal-treatment/welfare/breeding-\n   decision field (`mixedfarm.governor/log-record-forbidden-keys`:\n   `:planting-decision`, `:harvest-decision`, `:harvest-timing-\n   decision`, `:treatment-decision`, `:veterinary-treatment-order`,\n   `:welfare-disposition`, `:breeding-decision`, `:breeding-fitness-\n   decision`, `:cull-decision`) is a HARD, PERMANENT block -- this\n   actor never records a planting/harvest-timing decision, a treatment\n   decision, a welfare disposition, or a breeding decision, only\n   metadata check-ins.\n6. **Farm-schedule override forbidden** -- `:schedule-farm-operation`\n   is household labor/task scheduling logistics ONLY. Any proposal\n   carrying a planting/harvest directive, a treatment-directive, or a\n   farmer-judgment-override field\n   (`mixedfarm.governor/schedule-forbidden-keys`: `:planting-\n   directive`, `:harvest-directive`, `:crop-judgment-override`,\n   `:treatment-directive`, `:farmer-judgment-override`, `:livestock-\n   judgment-override`, `:breeding-directive`, `:cull-directive`,\n   `:welfare-disposition`) is a HARD, PERMANENT block -- this actor\n   never overrides the farmer's own judgment about their household's\n   crops or livestock, it only schedules which household member/task\n   is assigned to which farm-operation slot in advance.\n7. **Scope exclusion** -- independent, defense-in-depth layer on top of\n   checks 3/5/6: even for an otherwise-allowed op, any proposal whose\n   free text (`:rationale` or `:note`) names a finalization/execution\n   ACTION for a planting/harvest-timing agronomic decision, an\n   animal-treatment/welfare/breeding decision, or an override of the\n   farmer's own judgment about their household's crops or livestock\n   (`mixedfarm.governor/scope-excluded-terms`, e.g. \"proceed with the\n   planting decision\", \"determine the animal's fitness for breeding\",\n   \"override the farmer's crop or livestock judgment\", plus Japanese\n   equivalents for both halves) is a HARD, PERMANENT block, evaluated\n   unconditionally on content.\n\n### Two escalation gates, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-livelihood-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless\n  of confidence (governor's `always-escalate-ops`). This is the ONLY\n  channel by which the robot may surface an observation suggesting a\n  crop-failure, animal-welfare, injury-risk, or food-security concern\n  needs human attention -- it never resolves the observation itself,\n  and the robot's role ends at \"here is the planting/harvest/feeding/\n  roster/condition-check-in status,\" never \"here is whether to plant\n  or harvest now\" or \"here is whether the animal needs treatment or\n  should be bred.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-escalation-threshold` (300, inclusive boundary verified\n  by `ok-at-exact-supply-cost-threshold-boundary`; matches the\n  household-scale ceiling used by both sibling actors 6310/6320,\n  rather than a commercial depot's larger ceiling) -- always needs\n  human sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION,\n  not a hard block: an over-budget supply order (e.g. a full fencing\n  replacement) is not itself unsafe, unlike a planting/harvest-timing,\n  treatment/welfare/breeding, or farmer-judgment-override attempt,\n  which is why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft escalate\n  vs. structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"planting\", \"harvest\", \"breeding\", \"welfare\") can accidentally match\ninside the mock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate\nproposal, self-blocking the actor's own happy path. This risk is\nespecially sharp here because this actor's own op is literally named\n`:flag-livelihood-concern` and legitimate work-log proposals routinely\ndescribe bare nouns like \"planted maize row 3\" or \"animal showing\nsigns of injury\" -- a bare \"planting\"/\"animal\"/\"welfare\" term would\nself-trip on every single legitimate work-log or livelihood-flag\nproposal. `mixedfarm.governor/scope-excluded-terms` is phrased\nexclusively as finalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. \"proceed with the\nplanting decision\", \"determine the animal's fitness for breeding\",\n\"override the farmer's crop or livestock judgment\" -- the exact\nphrasing pattern given in the task's guidance on this known bug\nclass), never bare nouns, and `mixedfarm.governor-test/\ndefault-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-scope-exclusion` is a\ndedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed allowlist\n(covering both `:work-type :planting` and `:work-type :feeding-\nschedule` variants of `:log-work-record`, and the holding-present,\nholding-nil-crop, and holding-nil-welfare variants of\n`:flag-livelihood-concern`), driven through the default mock advisor,\nnever trips `:scope-excluded` or `:op-not-allowed`. The mock advisor's\nown default rationale template (\"documented <op> for holding <id>\" /\n\"documented <op> (no holding yet -- new-holding intake)\") was written\nto avoid any bare-noun collision by construction, mirroring 6320's\nproven-safe template rather than 6310's per-op template (which\nembedded richer bare-noun-laden text -- also safe there, but the\nsingle generic template is simpler to keep safe when combining two\nguardrail vocabularies). Green on first run this session -- the\nscope-exclusion self-trip guard itself was never tripped during\ndevelopment or verification.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"6330\" ...}` entry promoted\n  from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id` set to\n  the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-6330` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-6330`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately before\n  the edit to account for concurrent sibling landings in this same\n  retry batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 51 tests, 192 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy). No mojibake found in the fresh clone's\n  Japanese scope-exclusion strings.\n- No implementation bug was found during this session's verification\n  pass -- the test suite was green on first run against both the local\n  working tree and the fresh post-push clone.\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- Full doc set published: README.md, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md,\n  CONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md, SECURITY.md, LICENSE\n  (AGPL-3.0-or-later, byte-identical to the `cloud-itonami-isco-6320`/\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-6310` reference LICENSE text),\n  docs/business-model.md, docs/operator-guide.md, blueprint.edn.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Splitting this occupation into two separate actors (a crop half and\n  a livestock half) rather than one combined actor: rejected -- the\n  ISCO-08 6330 unit group is explicitly defined as farmers who BOTH\n  grow crops AND raise animals on the same subsistence holding; a\n  single holding registration and a single farmer identity govern both\n  activities in practice, so splitting the actor would misrepresent\n  the occupation and double the provenance-registration burden on the\n  same household for no safety benefit.\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-planting-decision`/\n  `:approve-treatment-decision` that is merely gated behind\n  `always-escalate-ops` (the shape a naive reading of \"gate high-stakes\n  actions behind human approval\" might suggest): rejected outright per\n  the task's explicit guidance and the `ADR-2607152500` precedent -- an\n  always-escalate op still means the *capability* to finalize an\n  agronomic or treatment/breeding decision exists in the code path and\n  is merely deferred to a human gate. For decisions carrying a\n  livelihood, welfare, AND agronomic-authority dimension all at once,\n  the capability itself must not exist. The only op related to\n  escalating a concern is `:flag-livelihood-concern`, which surfaces\n  the need for human attention and never itself decides, authorizes,\n  or defers a planting/harvest/treatment/breeding action for later\n  automatic execution.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"planting\" \"harvest\"\n  \"breeding\" \"welfare\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known\n  self-trip bug class described above -- this actor's own op is\n  literally named `:flag-livelihood-concern`, and legitimate work-log\n  proposals routinely describe planting/harvest/animal-condition bare\n  nouns, so a bare-noun term would self-trip on the actor's own happy\n  path.\n- Merging `farmer` and `holding` into a single entity (only checking\n  one provenance record): rejected -- the holding-mismatch check\n  requires two independently registered-AND-verified entities so that\n  a holding record a farmer is not assigned to is caught even if both\n  records individually pass verification.\n- A numeric confidence-only gate for `:log-work-record` /\n  `:schedule-farm-operation` (no forbidden-keys content checks):\n  rejected as insufficient -- confidence measures the advisor's\n  certainty about the *proposal it chose to make*, not whether that\n  proposal smuggles forbidden content; a high-confidence advisor could\n  still misuse an allowed op's field shape to carry a forbidden\n  agronomic, treatment, or breeding decision, which is exactly why\n  checks 5/6 exist as independent, content-based, permanent HARD\n  blocks regardless of confidence.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- planting/harvest/feeding-schedule/animal-\n   condition-check-in metadata only (work id, work type, detail,\n   timestamp). Cannot carry a planting/harvest-timing decision, a\n   treatment decision, a welfare disposition, or a breeding decision\n   (HARD-blocked by check 5, `:work-record-decision-forbidden`, on a\n   closed forbidden-key list). Not an agronomic or animal-treatment/\n   welfare/breeding decision in any respect -- it is a records-\n   management action indistinguishable in kind from a farm clerk's log\n   entry.\n2. **`:schedule-farm-operation`** -- household labor/task scheduling\n   logistics only (task id, proposed time, assignee). Cannot carry a\n   planting/harvest directive, a treatment directive, or a real-time\n   farmer-judgment override (HARD-blocked by check 6, `:farm-schedule-\n   override-forbidden`, on a closed forbidden-key list). Does not\n   direct, override, or draw any conclusion about a live agronomic or\n   treatment/breeding decision -- it is a calendar action deciding in\n   advance which household member/task is assigned to which farm-\n   operation slot.\n3. **`:flag-livelihood-concern`** -- surfaces a crop-failure/animal-\n   welfare/injury-risk/food-security concern to a human; ALWAYS\n   escalates (governor's `always-escalate-ops`, unconditionally, at\n   every confidence level), NEVER auto-commit eligible. This is the\n   ONLY channel through which such a concern may be surfaced, and it\n   explicitly never resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself --\n   the human reviews and acts entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- seeds/feed/tools procurement\n   proposal (item, cost, vendor). Gated by ordinary cost-ceiling\n   escalation (above 300 always escalates). Not an agronomic or\n   animal-welfare/breeding action of any kind -- it is a purchasing-\n   logistics action for seed/feed/tool upkeep.\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na planting/harvest-timing agronomic decision, an animal-treatment/\nwelfare/breeding decision, or an override of the farmer's own judgment\nabout their household's crops or livestock -- these categories are\nstructurally absent, not gated, confirmed by: (a) the closed four-op\nallowlist itself (`mixedfarm.governor/closed-op-allowlist`), (b)\nseventeen concretely-named forbidden-op fixtures that are rejected at\nboth the governor-unit level and the full-actor-graph level via a\nrogue advisor (`mixedfarm.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`mixedfarm.governor-test/every-scope-excluded-op-name-is-rejected`,\n`mixedfarm.actor-test/holds-every-scope-excluded-op-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`),\n(c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield (`mixedfarm.governor/out-of-scope?`,\n`mixedfarm.governor/scope-excluded-terms`), tested end-to-end through a\nrogue advisor and the full compiled graph\n(`mixedfarm.actor-test/holds-a-scope-excluded-rationale-attempt-even-via-a-rogue-advisor`),\nand (d) two additional content-shape guardrails preventing an\notherwise-allowed op from smuggling forbidden substance through its own\nfield shape (checks 5/6 above), both independently proven end-to-end\nvia a rogue advisor driving the full compiled StateGraph\n(`mixedfarm.actor-test/holds-a-planting-decision-attempt`,\n`mixedfarm.actor-test/holds-a-treatment-decision-attempt`,\n`mixedfarm.actor-test/holds-a-farm-schedule-override-attempt`).\n"
